    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d244">

                <pb facs="0383" n="307" sameAs="frus:frus1935v02/pg307"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">862.20/803</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Dodd</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1866</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-03-20">March 20, 1935</date>.<lb/>[Received March
                        29.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I wish to express my thanks to the
                    Department for its Circular Telegram of March 18, 7 p.m.,<anchor n="48"
                        xml:id="d244-f1"/> received via the Paris Embassy, giving consensus of the
                    American press reaction to Hitler’s announcement that the Reich would increase
                    its national defense forces.</p>
                <p>This review of the American press is especially helpful to us here in view of the
                    efforts of the German press to make it appear that America, at the most, was
                    indifferent to the Chancellor’s action and inclined to accept it as inevitable
                    and justifiable in view of increased armaments of other nations.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">William E. Dodd</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
